{"id":55,"title":"Projectile Points","slug":"projectile-points","summary":"Projectile points preserve thousands of years of Indigenous skill, hunting technology, stone knowledge, movement, and exchange. Many pointed stone tools commonly called arrowheads were made for spears or atlatl darts rather than arrows.","content":"<h2>Stone Tools Across Generations</h2><p>Pointed stone tools made on Long Island are often called arrowheads, but <em>projectile points</em> is the more accurate general term. Many were fitted to spears or atlatl darts, while some also served as knives or other cutting tools. Small, unstemmed triangular points are the forms most often associated with bow-and-arrow technology, which became widespread much later in the island's long Indigenous history.<sup class=\"footnote-ref\" title=\"John A. Strong, The Algonquian Peoples of Long Island from Earliest Times to 1700 (1997), pp. 37 and 78.\">1</sup></p><p>Long Island has no natural chert deposits. Native toolmakers worked difficult local quartz and quartzite beach cobbles with great skill, while points made from chert, jasper, and other non-local stone also show movement and exchange across a wider region.<sup class=\"footnote-ref\" title=\"John A. Strong, The Algonquian Peoples of Long Island from Earliest Times to 1700 (1997), pp. 37-38; Edward S. Rutsch, 'An Analysis of the Lithic Materials Used in the Manufacture of Projectile Points in Coastal New York' (1970), reprinted in The Coastal Archaeology Reader (1978), pp. 182-193.\">2</sup></p><h2>Reading Point Types Carefully</h2><figure><a href=\"https://nativelongisland.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/03/Major-Aboriginal-Projectile-Points-In-New-York-State-Long-Islands-First-Inhabitants-William-Golder.jpg\" data-size=\"2048x842\" data-lbwps-width=\"1920\" data-lbwps-height=\"789\" data-lbwps-srcsmall=\"https://nativelongisland.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/03/Major-Aboriginal-Projectile-Points-In-New-York-State-Long-Islands-First-Inhabitants-William-Golder-300x123.jpg\"><img title=\"\" fetchpriority=\"high\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"data:image/gif;base64,R0lGODlhAQABAIAAAAAAAP///yH5BAEAAAAALAAAAAABAAEAAAIBRAA7\" data-src=\"https://nativelongisland.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/03/Major-Aboriginal-Projectile-Points-In-New-York-State-Long-Islands-First-Inhabitants-William-Golder.jpg\" alt=\"Major-Aboriginal-Projectile-Points-In-New-York-State-Long-Islands-First-Inhabitants-William-Golder Projectile Points\" width=\"2048\" height=\"842\" /><noscript><img title=\"\" fetchpriority=\"high\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://nativelongisland.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/03/Major-Aboriginal-Projectile-Points-In-New-York-State-Long-Islands-First-Inhabitants-William-Golder.jpg\" alt=\"Major-Aboriginal-Projectile-Points-In-New-York-State-Long-Islands-First-Inhabitants-William-Golder Projectile Points\" width=\"2048\" height=\"842\" /></noscript></a><figcaption>Major Projectile Points in New York State. Prepared by the Office of the State Archaeologist Text, layout and design by Phil Lord, Jr. From William Golder, Long Island's First Inhabitants 1983 pp. 86-87</figcaption></figure><p>Archaeologists compare shape, stone, manufacturing technique, and excavation context to identify point types and estimate age. A type name is a research classification, not the name of an Indigenous people or culture. Surface finds without a documented archaeological context cannot, by themselves, establish who made a point or exactly how it was used.</p><h2>Point Types and Archaeological Context</h2><figure><a href=\"https://nativelongisland.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/03/Clovis-Point-from-John-Strong-The-Alogonquian-Peoples-from-1700-pp-37.jpg\" data-size=\"208x396\" data-lbwps-width=\"208\" data-lbwps-height=\"396\" data-lbwps-srcsmall=\"https://nativelongisland.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/03/Clovis-Point-from-John-Strong-The-Alogonquian-Peoples-from-1700-pp-37-158x300.jpg\" data-lbwps-caption=\"Clovis projectile point from the Paleo-Indian Period. Image from John Strong&#8217;s The Algonquian Peoples of Long Island from Earliest Times to the 1700s, pp. 37\"><img title=\"\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"data:image/gif;base64,R0lGODlhAQABAIAAAAAAAP///yH5BAEAAAAALAAAAAABAAEAAAIBRAA7\" data-src=\"https://nativelongisland.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/03/Clovis-Point-from-John-Strong-The-Alogonquian-Peoples-from-1700-pp-37.jpg\" alt=\"Clovis-Point-from-John-Strong-The-Alogonquian-Peoples-from-1700-pp-37 Projectile Points\" width=\"165\" height=\"314\" /><noscript><img title=\"\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://nativelongisland.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/03/Clovis-Point-from-John-Strong-The-Alogonquian-Peoples-from-1700-pp-37.jpg\" alt=\"Clovis-Point-from-John-Strong-The-Alogonquian-Peoples-from-1700-pp-37 Projectile Points\" width=\"165\" height=\"314\" /></noscript></a><figcaption>Clovis projectile point from the Paleo-Indian Period. Image from John Strong's The Algonquian Peoples of Long Island from Earliest Times to the 1700s, pp. 37</figcaption></figure><p>Clovis Points were used for about 2,000 years by hunting and gathering bands across North America. The preferred material in North America for these points was \"chert,\" a subvitrious variety of quartz with a slightly lower percentage of silicon than flint.<sup><a href=\"#footnote_1_1699\" title=\"John Strong, The Algonquian Peoples of Long Island from Earliest Times to the 1700s, pp. 37\">1</a></sup></p><figure><a href=\"https://nativelongisland.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/03/bare-island-point-from-william-a-ritchie-a-typology-and-nomenclature-1961.jpg\" data-size=\"259x434\" data-lbwps-width=\"259\" data-lbwps-height=\"434\" data-lbwps-srcsmall=\"https://nativelongisland.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/03/bare-island-point-from-william-a-ritchie-a-typology-and-nomenclature-1961-179x300.jpg\"><img title=\"\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"data:image/gif;base64,R0lGODlhAQABAIAAAAAAAP///yH5BAEAAAAALAAAAAABAAEAAAIBRAA7\" data-src=\"https://nativelongisland.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/03/bare-island-point-from-william-a-ritchie-a-typology-and-nomenclature-1961.jpg\" alt=\"bare-island-point-from-william-a-ritchie-a-typology-and-nomenclature-1961 Projectile Points\" width=\"171\" height=\"287\" /><noscript><img title=\"\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://nativelongisland.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/03/bare-island-point-from-william-a-ritchie-a-typology-and-nomenclature-1961.jpg\" alt=\"bare-island-point-from-william-a-ritchie-a-typology-and-nomenclature-1961 Projectile Points\" width=\"171\" height=\"287\" /></noscript></a><figcaption>Bare Island Point, from William A Ritchie's A Typology and Nomenclature For New York Projectile Points, 1961, pp. 14</figcaption></figure><p><strong>Bare Island Point's </strong>average size from 1.2 inches to 3.8 inches. Found at Archaic sites in the lower Susquehanna watershed, found more abundantly on the Islands of the Susquehanna rather than hilltop sites. Presumed to be part of a varied and complex Late Archaic period, as it is contemporaneous with the steatite bowl.</p><p>The points are also found in some abundance on Staten Island and Long Island, with a diminishing frequency northward up the Hudson Valley.<sup><a href=\"#footnote_2_1699\" title=\" William A Ritchie's A Typology and Nomenclature For New York Projectile Points, 1961, pp. 15\">2</a></sup></p><figure><a href=\"https://nativelongisland.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/03/orient-fishtail-point-from-william-a-ritchie-a-typology-and-nomenclature-1961.jpg\" data-size=\"257x538\" data-lbwps-width=\"257\" data-lbwps-height=\"538\" data-lbwps-srcsmall=\"https://nativelongisland.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/03/orient-fishtail-point-from-william-a-ritchie-a-typology-and-nomenclature-1961-143x300.jpg\"><img title=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"data:image/gif;base64,R0lGODlhAQABAIAAAAAAAP///yH5BAEAAAAALAAAAAABAAEAAAIBRAA7\" data-src=\"https://nativelongisland.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/03/orient-fishtail-point-from-william-a-ritchie-a-typology-and-nomenclature-1961.jpg\" alt=\"orient-fishtail-point-from-william-a-ritchie-a-typology-and-nomenclature-1961 Projectile Points\" width=\"153\" height=\"320\" /><noscript><img title=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://nativelongisland.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/03/orient-fishtail-point-from-william-a-ritchie-a-typology-and-nomenclature-1961.jpg\" alt=\"orient-fishtail-point-from-william-a-ritchie-a-typology-and-nomenclature-1961 Projectile Points\" width=\"153\" height=\"320\" /></noscript></a><figcaption><a href=\"#wiki/orient-transitional-period\">Orient</a> Fishtail Point, from William A Ritchie's A Typology and Nomenclature For New York Projectile Points, 1961, pp. 39</figcaption></figure><p><strong>Orient Fishtail Point </strong>length range from 1-3/16 to 4 inches, predominantly 2 to 2-1/2 inches with a <a href=\"#wiki/fishing\">fish</a> shape.</p><p>Associated with the Late Archaic and Transitional / <a href=\"#wiki/orient-transitional-period\">Orient period</a> into Early Woodland. This point can be found on eastern and southern New York, particularly the middle and lower Hudson Valley and Long Island.</p><p>On Long Island, the local points were made of quartz or quartzite pebbles.<sup><a href=\"#footnote_3_1699\" title=\" William A Ritchie's A Typology and Nomenclature For New York Projectile Points, 1961, pp. 39\">3</a></sup></p><figure><a href=\"https://nativelongisland.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/03/Steubenville-Lanceolate-Point-from-william-a-ritchie-a-typology-and-nomenclature-1961.jpg\" data-size=\"229x415\" data-lbwps-width=\"229\" data-lbwps-height=\"415\" data-lbwps-srcsmall=\"https://nativelongisland.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/03/Steubenville-Lanceolate-Point-from-william-a-ritchie-a-typology-and-nomenclature-1961-166x300.jpg\"><img title=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"data:image/gif;base64,R0lGODlhAQABAIAAAAAAAP///yH5BAEAAAAALAAAAAABAAEAAAIBRAA7\" data-src=\"https://nativelongisland.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/03/Steubenville-Lanceolate-Point-from-william-a-ritchie-a-typology-and-nomenclature-1961.jpg\" alt=\"Steubenville-Lanceolate-Point-from-william-a-ritchie-a-typology-and-nomenclature-1961 Projectile Points\" width=\"130\" height=\"236\" /><noscript><img title=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://nativelongisland.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/03/Steubenville-Lanceolate-Point-from-william-a-ritchie-a-typology-and-nomenclature-1961.jpg\" alt=\"Steubenville-Lanceolate-Point-from-william-a-ritchie-a-typology-and-nomenclature-1961 Projectile Points\" width=\"130\" height=\"236\" /></noscript></a><figcaption>Steubenville Lanceolate Point from William A Ritchie's A Typology and Nomenclature For New York Projectile Points, 1961, pp. 50</figcaption></figure><p><strong>Steubenville Lanceolate Point </strong>ranges from 1 to 3-3/16 inches. Described as problematical in the New York area, the points were also found as part of the Panhandle Archaic of Wester Virginia in association with Steubenville Stemmed Points.</p><p>Along with West Virginia, and parts of the upper Ohio Valley, central New York, Susquehanna Valley, Staten Island, upper Delaware Valley in New York, and eastern New York, the points can also be found on Long Island.<sup><a href=\"#footnote_4_1699\" title=\" William A Ritchie's A Typology and Nomenclature For New York Projectile Points, 1961, pp. 50\">4</a></sup></p><figure><a href=\"https://nativelongisland.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/03/SQUIBNOCKET-stemmed-point-from-william-a-ritchie-a-typology-and-nomenclature-1961.jpg\" data-size=\"208x453\" data-lbwps-width=\"208\" data-lbwps-height=\"453\" data-lbwps-srcsmall=\"https://nativelongisland.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/03/SQUIBNOCKET-stemmed-point-from-william-a-ritchie-a-typology-and-nomenclature-1961-138x300.jpg\"><img title=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"data:image/gif;base64,R0lGODlhAQABAIAAAAAAAP///yH5BAEAAAAALAAAAAABAAEAAAIBRAA7\" data-src=\"https://nativelongisland.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/03/SQUIBNOCKET-stemmed-point-from-william-a-ritchie-a-typology-and-nomenclature-1961.jpg\" alt=\"SQUIBNOCKET-stemmed-point-from-william-a-ritchie-a-typology-and-nomenclature-1961 Projectile Points\" width=\"138\" height=\"301\" /><noscript><img title=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://nativelongisland.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/03/SQUIBNOCKET-stemmed-point-from-william-a-ritchie-a-typology-and-nomenclature-1961.jpg\" alt=\"SQUIBNOCKET-stemmed-point-from-william-a-ritchie-a-typology-and-nomenclature-1961 Projectile Points\" width=\"138\" height=\"301\" /></noscript></a><figcaption>Squibnocket Stemmed Point from William A Ritchie's A Typology and Nomenclature For New York Projectile Points, 1961, pp. 126</figcaption></figure><p><strong>Squibnocket Stemmed Point </strong>is described as a small, thick, very narrow drill-like point, with marked tapered stems. Ranging from 7/8 of an inch to 2 inches in length and spike shaped.</p><p>The point has been found on Martha's Vineyard, and associated with the Wading River and Squibnocket Triangle Points from the Late Archaic stage. Ritchie describes the point as a variant of the Wading River Point. It is usually made of quartz with evidence of percussion chipping.</p><p>The point is common in southern New England and Long Island.<sup><a href=\"#footnote_5_1699\" title=\" William A Ritchie's A Typology and Nomenclature For New York Projectile Points, 1961, pp. 126\">5</a></sup></p><figure><a href=\"https://nativelongisland.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/03/SQUIBNOCKET-triangle-point-from-william-a-ritchie-a-typology-and-nomenclature-1961.jpg\" data-size=\"332x374\" data-lbwps-width=\"332\" data-lbwps-height=\"374\" data-lbwps-srcsmall=\"https://nativelongisland.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/03/SQUIBNOCKET-triangle-point-from-william-a-ritchie-a-typology-and-nomenclature-1961-266x300.jpg\"><img title=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"data:image/gif;base64,R0lGODlhAQABAIAAAAAAAP///yH5BAEAAAAALAAAAAABAAEAAAIBRAA7\" data-src=\"https://nativelongisland.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/03/SQUIBNOCKET-triangle-point-from-william-a-ritchie-a-typology-and-nomenclature-1961.jpg\" alt=\"SQUIBNOCKET-triangle-point-from-william-a-ritchie-a-typology-and-nomenclature-1961 Projectile Points\" width=\"144\" height=\"163\" /><noscript><img title=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://nativelongisland.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/03/SQUIBNOCKET-triangle-point-from-william-a-ritchie-a-typology-and-nomenclature-1961.jpg\" alt=\"SQUIBNOCKET-triangle-point-from-william-a-ritchie-a-typology-and-nomenclature-1961 Projectile Points\" width=\"144\" height=\"163\" /></noscript></a><figcaption>Squibnocket Triangle Point from William A Ritchie's A Typology and Nomenclature For New York Projectile Points, 1961, pp. 126</figcaption></figure><p><strong>Squibnocket Triangle Point</strong> range from 3/4 inches to 1-1/2 inches in length.</p><p>The Squibnocket Triangle Point is associated with the Squibnocket complex, but also present at the Wading River site on Long Island in association with Wading River points<sup><a href=\"#footnote_6_1699\" title=\"Ritchie, 1959\">6</a></sup>.</p><p>The points are commonly found in southern New England and in southeastern and southern New York, especially on Long Island.</p><figure><a href=\"https://nativelongisland.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/03/wading-river-point-from-william-a-ritchie-a-typology-and-nomenclature-1961.jpg\" data-size=\"251x393\" data-lbwps-width=\"251\" data-lbwps-height=\"393\" data-lbwps-srcsmall=\"https://nativelongisland.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/03/wading-river-point-from-william-a-ritchie-a-typology-and-nomenclature-1961-192x300.jpg\"><img title=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"data:image/gif;base64,R0lGODlhAQABAIAAAAAAAP///yH5BAEAAAAALAAAAAABAAEAAAIBRAA7\" data-src=\"https://nativelongisland.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/03/wading-river-point-from-william-a-ritchie-a-typology-and-nomenclature-1961.jpg\" alt=\"wading-river-point-from-william-a-ritchie-a-typology-and-nomenclature-1961 Projectile Points\" width=\"170\" height=\"266\" /><noscript><img title=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://nativelongisland.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/03/wading-river-point-from-william-a-ritchie-a-typology-and-nomenclature-1961.jpg\" alt=\"wading-river-point-from-william-a-ritchie-a-typology-and-nomenclature-1961 Projectile Points\" width=\"170\" height=\"266\" /></noscript></a><figcaption>Wading River Point from William A Ritchie's A Typology and Nomenclature For New York Projectile Points, 1961, pp. 126</figcaption></figure><p><strong>Wading River</strong> (narrow or small stemmed) <strong>points</strong>, much associated with the Late Archaic period, but shown to extend temporally into the Contact period<sup><a href=\"#footnote_7_1699\" title=\"Lavin, 1984, pp. 31\">7</a></sup> are abundant at sites along the Connecticut coast adjacent to <a href=\"#listing/manhansack-aqua-quash-awamock\">Shelter Island</a><sup class=\"footnote-ref\" title=\"Bourne 1972, p. 37; Pagoulatos 1983, p. 56.\">9</sup>, and are a common point type occurring here.</p><p>Ritchie designates the point as \"Wading River\" type because he first excavated a complex dominated by this point on the Wading River Site (traditional Algonquian name Pahquahkossit) on Long Island.<sup><a href=\"#footnote_8_1699\" title=\"Ritchie, 1959, pp. 78-88, 1969, pp. 241-242.\">8</a></sup></p><p>The point can also be found more specifically throughout New England, such as Massachusetts, Connecticut, Rhode Island and occurring farther north, especially in the Connecticut Valley, and ranging southward through eastern Pennsylvania into the Middle Atlantic area.</p><p>The majority of Wading River points are made of quartz pebbles, evidently by direct percussion chipping. The relatively few examples composed of other materials, especially those of flint, are better made, thinner and more symmetrical.<sup><a href=\"#footnote_9_1699\" title=\" William A Ritchie's A Typology and Nomenclature For New York Projectile Points, 1961, pp. 131\">9</a></sup></p><h2><span>Long Island Examples</span></h2><div><div><a href=\"#listing/winippague\"><img title=\"\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"data:image/gif;base64,R0lGODlhAQABAIAAAAAAAP///yH5BAEAAAAALAAAAAABAAEAAAIBRAA7\" data-src=\"https://nativelongisland.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/08/Arch_LongIsland_NY-Winippague-Site-Arrowheads.jpg\" alt=\"Arch_LongIsland_NY-Winippague-Site-Arrowheads Projectile Points\" /><noscript><img title=\"\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://nativelongisland.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/08/Arch_LongIsland_NY-Winippague-Site-Arrowheads.jpg\" alt=\"Arch_LongIsland_NY-Winippague-Site-Arrowheads Projectile Points\" /></noscript></a></p><p><a href=\"#listing/winippague\">Winippague</a></p><p>Bifacial tool/projectile points found at Winippague. Photo courtesy the Smithsonian Institution</div></div><div></div><div><div><a href=\"#listing/raconkamuck\"><img title=\"\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"data:image/gif;base64,R0lGODlhAQABAIAAAAAAAP///yH5BAEAAAAALAAAAAABAAEAAAIBRAA7\" data-src=\"https://nativelongisland.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/03/Bassford-Hawkins-arrowhead-from-number-57-ny-bulletin-pp.-3.jpg\" alt=\"Bassford-Hawkins-arrowhead-from-number-57-ny-bulletin-pp.-3 Projectile Points\" /><noscript><img title=\"\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://nativelongisland.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/03/Bassford-Hawkins-arrowhead-from-number-57-ny-bulletin-pp.-3.jpg\" alt=\"Bassford-Hawkins-arrowhead-from-number-57-ny-bulletin-pp.-3 Projectile Points\" /></noscript></a></p><p><a href=\"#listing/raconkamuck\">Raconkamuck | Lake Ronkonkoma</a></p><p>Bassford Hawkins projectile point, from Walter Saxon The Bulletin - Number 57 1973 pp. 3</div></div><div></div><div><div><a href=\"#listing/manhansack-aqua-quash-awamock\"><img title=\"\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"data:image/gif;base64,R0lGODlhAQABAIAAAAAAAP///yH5BAEAAAA